    "0.0.3": {
        "info": {
            "author": "Felix Lorenz",
            "author_email": "felix@okio.ai",
            "bugtrack_url": null,
            "classifiers": [
                "Development Status :: 3 - Alpha",
                "Intended Audience :: Developers",
                "License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License",
                "Operating System :: OS Independent",
                "Programming Language :: Python :: 3",
                "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.10",
                "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.8",
                "Programming Language :: Python :: 3.9",
                "Topic :: Multimedia :: Sound/Audio"
            ],
            "description": "# Nendo\n\n[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT) [![Twitter](https://img.shields.io/twitter/url/https/twitter.com/okio_ai.svg?style=social&label=Follow%20%40okio_ai)](https://twitter.com/okio_ai) [![](https://dcbadge.vercel.app/api/server/XpkUsjwXTp?compact=true&style=flat)](https://discord.gg/XpkUsjwXTp)\n\n## What is Nendo?\n\nNendo is an open-source generative AI tool suite that reimagines music. It provides an extensible **plugin architecture** with generative AI capabilities that are useful for artists and can be combined in an easy and intuitive way.\n\n## Installation\n\n```\npip install nendo\n```\n\n## Getting started\n\nIn general, nendo makes it easy to create a generative music composition (called **Nendo score**) in just a few lines of code. The following examples are meant to introduce the basic concepts. For a more involved example of a Nendo score, you can take a look at the example provided in `examples/polymath.py`.\n\n**Load track from file and Segment**\n\nFor this example, we need to install the nendo plugin `sf_segmenter` via:\n\n```\npip3 install nendo_sf_segmenter\n```\n\nThen, the `sf_segmenter` plugin is loaded automatically, upon Nendo's initialization (unless it is blacklisted):\n\n```\nfrom nendo import Nendo\n\n# initialize Nendo\n# this loads any installed and non-blacklisted plugin packages\nnendo_instance = Nendo()\n\ntrack = nendo_instance.load_track_from_file(\n    '/path/to/track.mp3'\n    metadata = { 'Note': '2023 remix' }\n)\n\n# segment track\nnendo_instance.sf_segment(track)\n\n# show track info - includes segment info\nprint(track)\n```\n\n### Notebook-style usage\n\nNendo can also be used intruitively from within Jupyter-notebook like environments. Just initialize nendo and set up a database backend in the first cell, then continue calling the nendo functions as you jam with your music library.\n\n### Interactive (DJ-style) usage\n\nComing soon\n\n## Plugins\n\n### Plugin architecture\n\nTODO describe\n\n### Installing a plugin\n\nTo install a plugin, simply install the corresponding package with your favorite package manager, e.g. pip. Note that in order to be picked up by Nendo, the plugin name must start with the prefix `nendo_` ([flask-style](https://tedboy.github.io/flask/flask_doc.extensiondev.html)):\n\n```\npip3 install nendo_myplugin\n```\n\n### Writing a plugin\n\nTo write a plugin, we recommend looking at the simple example plugin provided by okio, or get inspired by one of the many plugins out there!\n\n### Publishing a plugin\n\nTo publish a plugin, simply build and ship a package to your favorite package manager and/or enter the plugin's source repository in the following list: TODO link\n\n## Contributing\n\nThe most straight-forward way to contribute to nendo is to develop a Plugin. But of course we are also happy about any improvements and extensions of the core framework. For more information on how to help develop nendo, see TODO link\n\n1. Install poetry, see https://python-poetry.org/docs/#installing-with-the-official-installer\n\n2. Install the nendo package using poetry\n\n```\npoetry install\n```\n\n3. Done! Now you can `import nendo` and start developing!",
